 'Data (Q1 deblend with 1" templates, matched coarse beam, Tb; spread of the template ensemble + 14% cross-band): CS 7-6/HCO+ 3-2 N 0.051+-0.014, S 0.056+-0.019 (S/N = 1.09+-0.48); CS 7-6/HCN 3-2 N 0.077+-0.016, S 0.149+-0.050 (S/N = 1.93+-0.75, i.e. HCN/CS is 1.9x lower at S, 1.2 sigma). At low J (Harada Table 13, 1.7"): CS(2-1)/HCO+(1-0) N 0.239+-0.003, S 0.261+-0.008 (S/N 1.09), HCN(1-0)/CS(2-1) N 4.03, S 2.47 (S/N 0.61+-0.03), HNC(1-0)/HCN(1-0) N 0.559, S 0.495 (HNC/HCO+ N 0.537, S 0.320). Model (CS 7-6 RADEX at the HCO+ Tkin, n; N(CS) solved from the observed CS/HCO+ ratio per posterior draw): S minus N log[N(HCN)/N(CS)] = -0.51 (-0.69..-0.32; 2.8 sigma) in class A, -0.11 (0.6 sigma) in B, +0.16 (0.2 sigma) in F2; log[N(HCO+)/N(CS)] -0.26 (1.3 sigma) / +0.02 / -0.20. Verdict: from CS 7-6 alone UNDECIDED (model dependent, <=1.2 sigma in the data, 0.2-2.8 sigma in the models). The low-J literature data are model-light and point the same way: HCO+ and CS track each other at N and S, HCN (and HNC) are lower at S, i.e. they FAVOUR an HCN (N-bearing) deficit over an HCO+ enhancement, tentatively, provided CS(2-1) and HCO+(1-0) are excited similarly at N and S (not tested). This replaces both earlier statements: the Q1 sentence "CS tracks HCO+ not HCN" is the above, weak (1.2 sigma) result, and the Q4b "CS/HCO+ 4-3 0.047 S vs 0.081 N (factor 1.7)" came from undeblended peak pixels of the 4.1x2.7" beam (N and S partly blended) and is withdrawn.',
