 'Verified from the paper text/tables (arXiv HTML parsed with Python): Harada+2018 Table 13 (1.7", N and S; rows in out/q4c/harada_table13_rows.csv, ratios in harada_ratios.csv) and Michiyama+2018 Table 4 (2.1x1.7": R_HCN/HCO+(1-0) N 0.94+-0.13, S 0.60+-0.08; HCO+(1-0)/CO(1-0) S nucleus 0.08+-0.01). Ours vs published: HCN/HCO+ 1-0 (Harada) N 0.961, S 0.645; 3-2 at the same 1.7": ours N 0.748, S 0.378, Harada N 0.780, S 0.406 (differences 4-7%). Harada abstract/text: HCN(1-0)/HCO+(1-0) lower at S than N, no enhancement at the AGN (agrees). Harada Tables 5 and 6 (rotation-diagram column densities and Tex; two N/Tex pairs are printed per species, the HTML parse lost their labels): HCN N 2.4e14 (Tex 6.0 K) and 1.5e14 (6.1 K) at N, 8.8e13 (5.6 K) and 4.9e13 (5.8 K) at S; HCO+ N 1.3e14 (6.5 K) and 1.0e14 (6.9 K), S 8.5e13 (6.7 K) and 4.3e13 (7.3 K). Hence Tex(HCN) < Tex(HCO+) by 0.5-1.5 K at both nuclei (sign as in class A; ours 7.0 vs 11 K in RADEX for the 3-2/2-1/4-3 ladders, different lines and method) and N(HCN)/N(HCO+) = 1.85 / 1.5 at N versus 1.04 / 1.14 at S, i.e. a thin-LTE column ratio lower at S by 1.8 / 1.3, matching the 1.8-3x HCN-column change found in the N-vs-S fit. CH3OH Tex 15 K (N), 18.8/9.2 K (S) vs our CH3OH Trot 8-10 K. Abstract level only (tables NOT read): Ledger+2021 (HCN 3-2/1-0 ~0.3-0.4 in NGC 3256; ours 0.31-0.36 from Harada, 0.32-0.36 with our 3-2: agree), Nishimura+2024 (HCN/HCO+ 3-2 0.4-2.3, mean 1.1 in 23 U/LIRGs; non-LTE abundance interpretation), Sato+2022 (H2S/HCN 2-3x above the sample median in NGC 3256), Pereira-Santaella+2024, Brunetti+2021/22, Aalto+2015, Sakamoto+2010. NOT verified: HCN vib/ground, SO/HCO+, CH3OH/HCO+ in NGC 253, Arp 220, NGC 1068, so our limits are not placed against them; no shock-free starburst SO/HCO+ benchmark was taken from a paper.',
